WEBVTT

00:00:00.792 --> 00:00:03.750
This is a Final Project
Pitch Film created by

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:08.125
Ethan Gomes. The project is
called DataSentry, and its

00:00:08.125 --> 00:00:09.750
first pieces already run.

00:00:10.292 --> 00:00:13.333
The question isn't whether Claude
can write data checks.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.000
It's whether anyone can
measure what they miss.

00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:19.542
In plain words: companies set
rules to catch bad

00:00:19.542 --> 00:00:22.500
data, like a taxi fare below zero.

00:00:22.833 --> 00:00:26.250
DataSentry tests those rules
the way you'd test a

00:00:26.667 --> 00:00:30.125
spell-checker: hide some mistakes on
purpose, then count which

00:00:30.125 --> 00:00:32.375
ones it catches, and which slip through.

00:00:33.292 --> 00:00:35.667
DataSentry has an AI write
data checks for a

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:39.292
table, then scores those checks
by planting defects they

00:00:39.292 --> 00:00:40.125
have to catch.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:42.333
When it's done, it will be a public GitHub

00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:43.917
repo with three parts:

00:00:44.208 --> 00:00:47.458
a part that writes checks, a
defect planter, and

00:00:47.458 --> 00:00:49.542
a scorecard of what was
caught and what was

00:00:49.542 --> 00:00:52.125
missed. All three have already run.

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:53.833
It starts from public data.

00:00:54.083 --> 00:00:56.875
My first run used New York
City yellow taxi

00:00:56.875 --> 00:01:01.125
trips from January twenty twenty-
five, sampled to ten thousand

00:01:01.125 --> 00:01:01.625
rows.

00:01:01.917 --> 00:01:03.875
Under the hood, part one.

00:01:03.875 --> 00:01:05.875
Python and pandas load the table.

00:01:06.208 --> 00:01:09.375
A planter script makes
copies, breaks each one a

00:01:09.375 --> 00:01:12.250
different way, five ways in
all, and writes an

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.667
answer key that no check ever reads.

00:01:14.833 --> 00:01:19.042
Part two. Claude, Opus five
point five, saw only

00:01:19.042 --> 00:01:22.167
the column names and types, and
wrote pandera checks

00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:23.708
under a prompt contract.

00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:27.000
Each check runs on the
clean copy and every

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.375
broken copy, and a scorer
compares the flags with

00:01:30.375 --> 00:01:33.708
the answer key, one defect type at a time.

00:01:33.708 --> 00:01:34.917
Why would anyone care?

00:01:35.458 --> 00:01:38.208
I ran one rule, fare can't be negative, in

00:01:38.208 --> 00:01:40.167
two tools data teams use.

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:44.083
In dbt, it passed the clean
copy and failed

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.292
twenty-five rows of the broken one.

00:01:46.292 --> 00:01:48.958
In Great Expectations, with
the free G X Core,

00:01:48.958 --> 00:01:53.500
the same: pass, then twenty-
five unexpected values.

00:01:53.833 --> 00:01:58.291
Its paid GX Cloud adds ExpectAI,
which suggests checks for

00:01:58.291 --> 00:02:01.083
you. I tested that same idea with Claude.

00:02:01.583 --> 00:02:04.416
But I had planted twenty-five
trips that end before

00:02:04.416 --> 00:02:07.625
they start. None of the three
tools flagged them,

00:02:07.625 --> 00:02:09.708
because no rule asked.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.583
DataSentry tells a data engineer
which bad rows their

00:02:13.583 --> 00:02:14.666
checks let through.

00:02:14.708 --> 00:02:17.791
Who opens it? A hiring manager for a data

00:02:17.791 --> 00:02:22.000
engineering role. One command shows
each defect type, caught

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.333
or missed. My one hand-written
rule caught two of

00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:29.291
five. Claude's first checks caught
all five, but rejected

00:02:29.333 --> 00:02:30.916
two hundred twenty good trips.

00:02:31.333 --> 00:02:34.541
Its revision fixed that, and
missed every negative fare.

00:02:34.625 --> 00:02:35.500
What does it prove?

00:02:36.041 --> 00:02:38.583
Four skills from this
class, each with a run

00:02:38.583 --> 00:02:42.708
behind it: a prompt contract,
a two-round loop, a

00:02:42.875 --> 00:02:45.500
clean-copy control, and a
catch rate per defect.

00:02:45.958 --> 00:02:49.041
I test an AI's output
instead of trusting it.

00:02:49.041 --> 00:02:53.166
In scope: one table, five defect
types, one scorecard.

00:02:53.416 --> 00:02:55.000
Out of scope, on purpose:

00:02:55.375 --> 00:02:57.208
orchestration, so no Airflow.

00:02:57.708 --> 00:03:00.333
The first pieces have run,
and each run saves

00:03:00.333 --> 00:03:02.041
its scorecard with the date.

00:03:02.083 --> 00:03:03.166
What could sink it?

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:06.250
Checks that are too strict,
or too loose, like

00:03:06.375 --> 00:03:09.333
Claude's two rounds. So the
clean copy and the

00:03:09.333 --> 00:03:12.250
broken copies are both
required, every time.

00:03:12.416 --> 00:03:16.541
Why this project? In my Food
Inspections Dataset Project,

00:03:16.624 --> 00:03:18.916
my checks let bad rows
through, and I didn't

00:03:18.916 --> 00:03:20.541
know which ones until later.

00:03:21.083 --> 00:03:23.249
DataSentry measures exactly that.

00:03:23.583 --> 00:03:28.624
It's a mix: pandera, dbt, Great
Expectations, and prompting

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:29.833
Claude are new to me.

00:03:30.249 --> 00:03:33.124
Working with messy public data,
I've done before, in

00:03:33.124 --> 00:03:33.958
that project.

00:03:34.083 --> 00:03:36.708
Where this stands, October twenty-fifth.

00:03:36.708 --> 00:03:40.916
What exists: this pitch, the five-
defect planter, the scorer,

00:03:40.916 --> 00:03:43.791
and two rounds of checks
written by Claude, all

00:03:43.791 --> 00:03:45.874
run on real taxi data.

00:03:46.124 --> 00:03:48.999
Still to build: the public
repo, and a loop

00:03:48.999 --> 00:03:50.499
that runs on its own.

00:03:50.541 --> 00:03:53.041
That's DataSentry. I'm Ethan Gomes.

00:03:53.208 --> 00:03:54.166
Thanks for watching.
